cartridge "RASHGAR/C.M.BLAKEMORE/1988"

' RASHGAR - MASTER OF DIMENSION X
' A ColecoVision game based on "Rashgar" by Cleveland M. Blakemore, Ahoy!
' June 1988 (Commodore 128, BASIC 7.0 with a small machine-language
' interrupt routine). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' listing, orig/rashgar.bas.txt; C-128 programs do not run in the toolbox's
' C-64 emulator), the game is built for the ColecoVision: Rashgar's face
' (the listing's four mouth frames) in tiles at the centre of the shield
' rings, smooth sprites, an eye beam drawn in tiles, sound effects and an
' original title tune.
'
' The game: you are Rashgar, a great face floating in Dimension X. Five
' aggressors (nested turning squares) drift about; steer your sight with
' the stick and press a button to fire the beam from your eyes to the
' sight. An aggressor in the sight is destroyed and comes back later from
' the edge of space. An aggressor that touches your face costs a life;
' three are lost and the game is over.
'
' From the original: the shield rings drawn one by one with a rising tone
' at the start (the listing's five circles at radii 20, 55, 90, 125, 160,
' scaled; the colours of its C(0)-C(3) table); the face with four mouth
' frames chewing (the interrupt routine's pointer cycle) and the pupils as
' a separate sprite that glows while firing; the box sight; five aggressors
' (sprites 4-8) of three nested squares turning, eight frames; the beam
' drawn from the eyes to the sight and erased; the main loop's rule that
' each pass one aggressor picked at random gets a new random direction; a
' hit only when the sight is on an aggressor as the beam fires; 100 x level
' points a hit; a new level every 8 hits; the aggressors' speed rising at
' levels 4 and 8 (S = S + 1); three lives, the debris explosion when the
' face is touched, the aggressors sent back out after a lost life; GAME OVER
' in big letters flying in one by one from the right; the high score.
'
' Liberties: the rings are octagons (a circle would need more tile codes
' than a screen third has); the aggressors bounce off the edges of the
' visible area (the C-128 let sprites wander off into its 512-pixel
' sprite space and wrap) and a destroyed one comes back from a random edge
' after a short wait (it was parked off screen); the C-128's random walk
' rarely brought an aggressor to the face, so the game could last for ever
' with the sight held still: here a new direction is sometimes aimed at
' Rashgar (2 in 8 at level 1, one more in 8 every two levels, at most 6
' in 8),
' and newcomers enter aimed at him; the beam fires about five times a second
' while the button is held (the BASIC loop's own pace) and the sight stops
' while it fires; the button fires even while the stick is pushed (the
' listing fired only with the stick centred); with two aggressors in the
' sight the first one is hit (the listing's collision test hit neither);
' the sight touching the face does nothing (on the C-128 it cost a life, a
' side effect of its sprite collision test); the screen flash on a lost life
' pulses the backdrop at 1.5 Hz instead of flashing quickly; the sight moves
' 2 pixels a frame and the aggressors 1/4, 1/2 and 3/4 pixel, scaled from
' the C-128's MOVSPR speeds 8 and 1-3.
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick moves the sight, a button fires.
' On the title, up and down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128 (each
' screen third has its own set, VDP R3 = $FF); the frame loop is protected
' from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off after a wait, display off.
' Music first in the sound table, effects after; the title tune is played
' once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own xorshift random numbers.

